NNAAMMEE
       dnsblog - Postfix DNS allow/denylist logger

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ddnnssbblloogg [generic Postfix daemon options]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  ddnnssbblloogg(8)  server  implements an ad-hoc DNS allow/denylist lookup
       service. This may eventually be replaced by an UDP client that is built
       directly into the ppoossttssccrreeeenn(8) server.

PPRROOTTOOCCOOLL
       With  each  connection,  the  ddnnssbblloogg(8)  server  receives  a  DNS  al‐
       low/denylist  domain name, an IP address, and an ID.  If the IP address
       is listed under the DNS allow/denylist, the ddnnssbblloogg(8) server logs  the
       match  and  replies  with the query arguments plus an address list with
       the resulting IP addresses, separated by whitespace, and the reply TTL.
       Otherwise it replies with the query arguments  plus  an  empty  address
       list  and the reply TTL; the reply TTL is -1 if there is no reply, or a
       negative reply that contains no SOA record.   Finally,  the  ddnnssbblloogg(8)
       server closes the connection.

DDIIAAGGNNOOSSTTIICCSS
       Problems and transactions are logged to ssyyssllooggdd(8) or ppoossttllooggdd(8).

